  /**
   * And the four parts need ceilings, because the contract said where the parts go
   * but never where the work itself goes.
   *
   * Measured over three engineer sessions and the close comments on the same issues
   * (session JSON under `origin/atomaton-data`, comment bodies from the GitHub API;
   * lengths in characters):
   *
   *   issue  whole  What I concluded  How I know  could not establish  next
   *   #2    23,895  17,983           2,435       2,770                707
   *   #3    12,517  (no four headings at all)
   *   #4    11,191  (no four headings at all)
   *
   * #2's `What I concluded` held six headings that were the deliverable the issue
   * asked for. #3 and #4 moved the deliverable outside the four parts and barely
   * wrote the headings -- so the contract never decided where the artifact goes.
   *
   * Anchored sentences by part, #2's engineer (same regex style as the earlier
   * measurement): `How I know` 10/11 = 91%, `What you could not establish` 6/13 =
   * 46%. So the part worth narrowing is `How I know`, and the part whose ratio must
   * not be traded away is the one that goes missing -- which is why that one gets a
   * floor and no ceiling. Narrowing `How I know` lowers its own ratio by shrinking
   * the denominator; that is not a regression, and the metric to compare across this
   * change is anchored claims per report, not their share.
   *
   * The close comment shows the same judgement written twice: on #4 `reason` was
   * 1,320 of 2,947 characters (44.8%) above a 243-character conclusion saying the
   * same thing. `src/entrypoints/tools/mcp/atomaton.ts` already asks the same of
   * `launch_sub_agent`'s `summary`, so this is the existing direction rather than a
   * new one.
   *
   * A character ceiling in the tool schema was rejected: `request_close_issue` ends
   * the session, so an over-length argument would be a tool error demanding a rewrite
   * as the last thing a run does, and the numbers worth capping are per part, which a
   * schema cannot see. Dropping the fixed four parts was rejected too: the `no-report`
   * mechanism depends on that shape. Pinned as single-line fragments, because these
   * files are wrapped prose.
   */
  test("the report contract puts a ceiling on three of its parts and says where the work goes", () => {
